   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the executive law, in relation to increasing the threshold for proof of financial difficulty",
    "basePrintNo": "S4913",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4913-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 43,
       "fullName": "Kathleen A. Marchione",
       "imgName": "435_kathleen_a._marchione.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 435,
       "sessionMemberId": 699,
       "sessionYear": 2015,
       "shortName": "MARCHIONE"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §631, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4913\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the executive law, in relation to\nincreasing the threshold for proof of financial difficulty\n\nPurpose of the Bill:\n\nThe purpose of this measure is to increase the threshold for proof of\nfinancial difficulty from claim totals of $5,000 or more to claim totals\nof $10,000 or more.\n\nSummary of Provisions:\n\nSection one of this measure amends the opening sentences of paragraph a\nof subdivision 6 of section 632 of the Executive Law. The threshold for\nproof of financial difficulty for claims is raised from claim totals of\n$5,000 or more to claim totals of $10,000 or more.\n\nSection two of this measure provides for the effective date.\n\nExisting Law:\n\nUnder current law, the Office is required to make a determination of\nfinancial difficulty in instances where claim expenses total $5,000 or\nmore.\n\nPrior Legislative History:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\nStatement in Support:\n\nThe $5,000 threshold for proof of financial difficulty was established\npursuant to Chapter 400 of the laws of 1991. As the cost of medical care\nand the general cost of living have steadily increased since this thres-\nhold was established, this amount has remained unchanged for well over\ntwo decades. The information required to establish financial difficulty\ncan be burdensome to an already traumatized claimant population. Addi-\ntionally, the Office's analysis of the information adds to claims' proc-\nessing time. The collecting and analyzing of this information is argu-\nably for marginal gain to the agency, as there are few, if any,\ninstances of the agency determining that financial difficulty has not\nbeen met. Therefore, claimants must endure a cumbersome process of\ncollecting personal financial information that, in the end, will have\nlittle, if any, bearing on the outcome of their claim or benefit to the\nstate.\n\nWhile the Office understands the perception in requiring such proof, it\nwould be a great benefit to raise this threshold, thereby requiring such\nanalyses with less frequency.\n\nBudget Implications:\n\nIt has been determined that these changes will result in minimal fiscal\nimpact. Compensation costs are estimated to increase by no more than\n$400,000, while the fiscal impact on operational expenses of the Office\nwill decrease, due to an increased administrative efficiency. Victim\ncompensation expenses are funded through a combination of State (Crimi-\nnal Justice Improvement Act or CJIA) and federal funding. Pursuant to\nthe federal (Victims of Crime Act or VOCA) grant received by the State\nfor such compensation, any increase to the overall compensation expenses\nof the OVS will be offset by a subsequent increase in federal compen-\nsation funding.\n\nLocal Impact:\n\nNone\n\nEffective Date:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately, and shall apply to all claims\nfiled on and after such date it shall become a law.\n\n",
